WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.429 align:start position:0%
 
my name is paulie murray  and my field of concentration has been  human rights in a society dominated by  the ideas  that blacks were inferior to whites  and women were inferior to men  paulie was a writer a lawyer a priest a  poet  paulie murray was not just an amazing  lawyer or a badass feminist but also a  queer and non-binary person  [Music]  we first heard about paulie murray from  justice ruth bader ginsburg when we were  making documentary rbg and when we  started looking into paulie's background  we were just flabbergasted any  innovative idea  to change the world for the better  paulie seemed to be thinking of it like  10 or 15 years before it occurred to  anybody else paulie had definite ideas  sometimes  ideas other people disagreed with  paulie was way ahead of the times i  chose for my senior paper should plessy  versus ferguson be overruled my little  argument went to the supreme court  i was floored  that i'd never heard of pauly murray and  that i knew most people  had not heard of paulie so our approach  was to  not just find all of the accolades but  to really try to get it paulie's  personality  amazingly after production assistant  amira williams came across drafts of an  autobiography that paulie was working on  a close friend of paulie's was visually  impaired and so uh to get thoughts from  this partially blind friend  paulie had done 30 or 40 hours of tape  so we had this autobiography which was  so beautiful and then  we had this gift  of paulie reading it  in their own voice this is  chapter one page one  of an autobiography in manuscript betsy  west and julie cohen said to me  we have a lot of audio but we're not  sure how to tell the story visually  because there aren't many images of  these amazing stories that polly is  telling and i come from a background of  an artist i do collage art and i said  wait a minute we can build our own  visual language  and that kind of excited me because we  can really be creative with making it an  experience  as a cinematographer knowing that this  film was heavily based on archival  material i had really the conflict of  how can i bring any  mood to the visuals that would tie  things together and for me there was  like this whole idea of hearing stories  of people from the present time  connecting to paulie in the past  i went and read what i hadn't read and  then i realized oh my god  petersburg bus incident march  1940.  i did not start out to contest  segregation statutes there was just  nothing you could do but fight  paulie murray was the kind of person who  saw an injustice  and could not help  trying to do something about it the  scene where polly murray is arrested on  a bus for not giving up her seat to a  white person there are no images of  polly murray being arrested on that bus  it just doesn't exist  there was no movement at the time when  paulie was refusing to move to the back  of the bus holly was acting alone but  that did not stop paulie  this was  15 years before rosa parks  refused to take her seat in the back of  the bus  so  the challenge was we had this audio of  polly telling the story of the bus but  what do we see what is paulie murray's  point of view and what if we could be on  that bus  my friend and i  were  traveling from new york down to durham  to visit my two aunts for easter we  found this old promotional video from  greyhound that had just a whole journey  on the greyhound bus and so  we approached the scene that way of  showing passengers showing rural  backgrounds because of where polly was  traveling we wanted it to play like a  scene so that you feel like you're on  the bus even though you never saw the  arrest you never saw paulie murray but  hopefully  you felt it happen  [Music]  i maintain that the  the tool of  teachers lawyers  and ministers  is the same thing words communication  julie  stumbled upon a videotape at the  schlesinger library and even though the  quality was degraded we were so excited  it was very much our goal for  viewers to understand not only paulie's  accomplishments but really kind of feel  paulie as a human being i think that  having the audio tapes the videotapes  allowed us to present paulie as a real  person and we really tried to lean into  who was paulie  sit down lie down  lie down  wonderful  he has to be in everything  in fiction there's a certain level of  hyperbole that you could actually add to  the music whereas in documentaries  everything has to have an element of  truth to it so the music has to support  that as well i always think about  what piece of the story what piece of  the environment can i bring into the  score and in this case it was a  typewriter  [Music]  and i used it as a percussive element in  the background to move the music forward  but also feel very organic to who paulie  was  i think we're in a time a very  radical change  i do think that we're going through a  time of reconsidering our history and  that really connected us  to paulie's story  this story kind of allows you not only  to learn about this magnificent figure  in american history but also maybe to  rethink the way we think about american  history in a broader sense paulie murray  is the perfect example of a person who  should actually be a household name who  should be included in our textbooks but  isn't and it's not like paulie murray is  missing and then the story is complete  there are so many other people who have  made such great contributions who we  should also be including  when we were making this film i was  always considering the person who was  struggling with something that polly  struggled with so that they could  understand the fight may be different  the language may be different but this  isn't new and polly murray is a person  that changed the world  i'm hoping that as people watch this  film  they'll really get a chance to  have pride in what paulie did  but at the same time ask themselves how  can i do better we learned so much in  making this film we wanted to have  paulie's compelling story out there and  people to be fascinated by this person  we think of this film as the beginning  of a conversation let's like delve in  and start learning if this country is to  survive  we must live together in harmony  [Music]  you

